WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.020
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Google Cloud offers

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:03.240
a wealth of managed
services and serverless

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.590
options for your workloads.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.360
But many mission
critical services

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.340
use compute
infrastructure directly

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.800
and run on Google
Compute Engine instances.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.740
How can we improve observability
for those workloads?

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.440
Today, I'm going to show you
how to use the Cloud Operations

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:17.760
agent to do just that.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.610
Welcome to Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.792
First, what are agents,
and why do we need them?

00:00:27.792 --> 00:00:29.750
Here is an example of an
infrastructure summary

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:33.090
dashboard for a project
with a few GCE VMs in it.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.360
Notice that we're not
getting any data about how

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.400
our instances are using memory.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.110
That's because the
VM hypervisor only

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.450
knows how much memory
is allocated to each VM,

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:43.160
not how much of the
allocated memory

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.110
the VM is actually using.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:47.270
This is just one example
of data that can only

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.430
be gathered by a process
running in the guest VM,

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.212
such as an agent.

00:00:51.212 --> 00:00:52.670
There are other
benefits of running

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.740
the Ops Agent inside the VM.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.050
It monitors your VM
instances without the need

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.990
for any additional
configuration after the install.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.870
It exposes many additional
metrics beyond memory,

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.750
and gives you better visibility
to CPU, disk, and network

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:06.590
performance.

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.260
And the Ops Agent unifies
gathering of metrics

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:12.303
and logs into a single agent.

00:01:12.303 --> 00:01:14.220
If you've been using
Google Cloud for a while,

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.530
you're probably familiar
with the Legacy monitoring

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.830
and logging agents.

00:01:17.830 --> 00:01:19.470
However, at this
point, the Ops Agent

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.483
should be used in all cases.

00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:24.400
Now that we've covered
the benefits of agents,

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.350
let's have a look at how
to install the Ops Agent.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:28.850
I'm going to show
you how to install

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:30.140
the agent in three ways.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.870
First, we'll install
the agent on a single VM

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:34.640
using local commands.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.370
Then I'll show you how to
install it on a fleet of VMs

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.265
by using Agent policies.

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.900
And finally, we'll
automate Agent installation

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.190
using Terraform.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:46.070
Let's get into it.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.630
From the project
dashboard, let's go

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:51.300
to monitoring dashboards.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.190
Select the VM
Instances dashboard.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:59.950
Here, we can see that there's
a VM without any agents on it.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.060
Let's SSH.machine.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.740
Once the SSH connection
is established,

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:08.650
run the commands to download
and install the Ops Agent.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.180
We'll link to the documentation
that has the up to date

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.620
command in the episode notes.

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.710
After some time, you'll see an
installation succeeded message

00:02:22.710 --> 00:02:24.240
from the Cloud Ops Agent.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:30.180
At this point, we can
go back to our dashboard

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.730
and confirm that the VM is
reporting as having the latest

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.080
version of the Agent installed.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.610
We can then go to
the VM detail screen

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.160
and see that we're getting
agent metrics for that VM,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.920
including memory utilization
and details of CPU usage.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:51.600
So that's an easy way
to install the Cloud Ops

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.370
Agent on a single
virtual machine

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:56.370
by using an SSH connection
and installing the Agent

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.750
through the command line.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:02:59.980
That was easy enough.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:01.810
Now that the Agent
is installed, let's

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:03.393
have a look at all
the additional data

00:03:03.393 --> 00:03:05.930
it's gathering from our VM.

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.600
Let's go to our VM
instances and select our VM.

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:15.400
The observability
tab is a single place

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:18.010
to see monitoring and logging
data collected for this VM.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:22.940
In the Metrics section,
we can see lots of data,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.660
like memory and
disk utilization.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:27.160
This is just some
of the information

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:31.480
that's being collected by
our Agent running on the VM.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.190
The Processes
section shows what's

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.710
running on the VM and
resource utilization of all

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.005
the various processes.

00:03:37.005 --> 00:03:39.130
Again, this information is
only available by having

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:41.970
an Agent on the VM.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.570
The Memory section shows us how
memory is being used by the VM.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:48.510
And finally, the Log section
will show us all the logs

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.520
being collected from the VM.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.080
We can open these
in Logs Explorer

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.640
to better understand where
each of these is coming from.

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.000
So that's how all the
additional data being gathered

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.220
by the Agent is collected
in a single place

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:03.260
and made easy for you to use.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.570
The Ops Agent collects
a lot of valuable data.

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:08.520
But how can we install it at
scale across our VM fleet?

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.550
Let's have a look at
automating the installation

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:15.240
and maintenance of the Ops
Agent with agent policies.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.279
Let's start on the VM
instances dashboard.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:28.640
Here, we see that we have
multiple VMs with no Agent

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:29.990
installed on them.

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:31.640
We can install an
Agent on all of them

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.335
at once by using
an Agent policy.

00:04:35.857 --> 00:04:37.440
Let's have a look
at an example of how

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.937
to do that using G Cloud.

00:04:38.937 --> 00:04:41.520
We'll post a link to the details
of the command in the episode

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.240
notes.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.760
But here's an example that
will require installing the Ops

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.770
Agent and all the
instances in the specified

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:50.870
zone that match the operating
system and version filter.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:52.970
Note that you'll have to
enable the relevant APIs

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:54.720
in your project in
order for this to work.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.405
Again, refer to
the documentation

00:04:56.405 --> 00:04:57.155
for those details.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:04.550
After some time, the operation
will report a success.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:06.350
We can then go back
to our dashboard

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.200
and see that the VMs that match
our operating system filter

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:11.690
and reside in the zone we
specified for the policy

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:16.008
have the latest version of
the Agent installed on them.

00:05:16.008 --> 00:05:17.800
We can go to the details
for one of the VMs

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:20.092
and confirm that we're getting
Agent information there.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:25.580
So that's how you can use
Agent policies to ensure

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.160
that the Cloud Ops Agent is
installed and automatically

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:31.610
upgraded across a VM fleet.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.980
Using Agent policies to
automate Agent installation

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:36.590
and maintenance is much more
scalable and error proof

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:37.790
than doing it manually.

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:39.680
But even entering
commands like this

00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:41.870
is still prone to
input and human error.

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:44.450
Let's have a look at how to
turn Agent policy configuration

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:48.490
into code with Terraform.

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:52.280
Let's start by defining our
Agent policy using Terraform.

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:54.290
The Agent rule
section will define

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:57.290
which Agent we want to install,
and whether we want to enable

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:59.180
auto upgrading for it.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:06:01.970
The group labels, OS
types and zones filters,

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:05.520
will define which VMs are
subject to the policy.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.430
To create the policy, first
issue the Terraform validate

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:11.230
command to make sure
your code is correct.

00:06:11.230 --> 00:06:13.080
Once it's validated,
use the Terraform

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.450
apply command to create
your Agent policy

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:18.060
and confirm that the
operation is successful.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:19.830
Once the Apply
command is finished,

00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.920
run the G Cloud describe command
to validate that the policy has

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.320
been successfully created.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.502
Finally, verify that the
policy has been applied,

00:06:27.502 --> 00:06:29.960
and that the Agent has been
installed on the instances that

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:33.070
are subject to the policy.

00:06:33.070 --> 00:06:34.310
Thanks for joining me today.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:37.060
We covered the Ops Agent, how
it differs from the Legacy

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:40.360
monitoring and logging agents,
manual and automated Agent

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:42.712
installation, and the data
that the Agent provides

00:06:42.712 --> 00:06:44.920
to help you improve the
observability if your service

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:46.465
is running on GCE.

00:06:46.465 --> 00:06:48.340
I hope this helps you
find issues in your VMs

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.320
faster and keep
your users happy.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:51.723
Thanks so much for watching.

00:06:51.723 --> 00:06:53.890
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:56.680
on more Engineering for
Reliability with Google Cloud.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:58.590
See you soon.

